    "result_text": "Build succeeds. The full suite ran (AcceptanceTest is the project's test class) \u2014 all green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization for owner creation with minimal structural impact:\n\n**New file** \u2014 `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`: a small, self-contained pipeline step (single method, no surrounding class complexity) that mutates the published `OwnerFieldsDto` in place, mirroring the existing `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`/`NormalizeOwnerEmail` steps. It trims, collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`) token-by-token.\n\n**One edit** \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`: wired the step in after `normalizeEmail`, before the household checks and `BuildOwner`.\n\n**Why this satisfies the whole spec without touching the household functions:**\n- **Store/return normalized:** the step runs before `BuildOwner`, so the entity is persisted and returned with the normalized address.\n- **Comparisons use the normalized form:** because the address is normalized before `RejectDuplicateHousehold`, `BuildOwner`, and `AssignHousehold` run, both duplicate detection and the shared household id already operate on normalized stored data \u2014 no change needed in those classes.\n- **Reject blank-after-normalize:** `RequireOwnerFields` runs first and rejects whitespace-only addresses via `isBlank()` \u2192 400 (abbreviation expansion never turns a non-blank address blank, so this is exactly the blank-after-normalize set).\n\nThis keeps the change to 2 files, both with near-zero `WMC_other` (a brand-new one-method class and a YAML wiring edit), rather than growing `RequireOwnerFields`, `RejectDuplicateHousehold`, or `AssignHousehold`.\n\nAll tests pass: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
